{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target claims the distinction between truly independent and apparently uncorrelated failures is 'revealed by the temporal and spatial distribution of recovery times.' But its own first countercondition defeats this: a shared cause can trigger failures in a staggered sequence that mimics random distribution. If a hidden shared cause produces staggered triggers AND staggered recoveries (because each node hits the bug under different load or at different rollouts), then the recovery-time distribution is indistinguishable from the independent case. Recovery-time distribution only catches synchronized shared causes, not shared causes with delayed or load-dependent onset — so the proposed evidential signal is neither necessary nor sufficient to distinguish the two failure modes.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A leap-second bug affects every decentralized node, but each node triggers it only when its local NTP sync crosses the boundary; recoveries also stagger as each operator independently patches. Recovery-time distribution looks random, matching the proposed 'truly independent' signature, yet the failures share one cause."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target argues against decentralization by claiming a high baseline of 'noise' failures makes correlated systemic failures harder to detect, but this conflates detectability of correlated failure with resilience to correlated failure. The problem asks which architecture is more resilient to correlated failure, not which is easier to monitor. A high noise floor could equally impair a large unified service's anomaly detection (volume of benign internal failures can mask systemic signals), so the argument does not establish that the large service is more resilient. It also never engages the problem's central requirement: distinguishing truly independent failures from apparently uncorrelated ones — the 'noise' it cites is assumed uncorrelated without any evidence criterion that would catch a hidden shared cause lurking within that noise.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A large unified service has thousands of internal component-level failures daily (benign retries, flapping replicas); a correlated config-rollout failure is masked by this noise until the whole cluster degrades. The decentralized network has comparable noise but diverse stacks, so the same rollout poison-pill does not propagate. Detectability is no better for the large service, yet it is the one that suffers the correlated collapse."}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target claims a large service is more resilient because its global control plane enables faster traffic evacuation, but its own second countercondition (poison pill passed with traffic) shows the evacuation mechanism can become the correlated-failure vector: shifting traffic to healthy segments injects the failure into them too. The argument therefore only works for failures that are not content-borne or config-borne. It also ignores that a global control plane is itself a shared cause that can turn an isolated fault into a synchronized one (its first countercondition), exactly the correlated-failure class the problem is concerned with. So the covering principle it relies on — 'central coordination improves resilience' — fails precisely for the correlated-failure regime the problem targets.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A large service detects a failing segment and evacuates traffic to redundant segments; the traffic carries a malformed request that triggers a shared parser bug in all segments, so evacuation synchronously propagates the failure rather than containing it. A decentralized network with heterogeneous parsers would degrade partially instead."}],"target_alias":"SRC_003"},{"attack":false,"case":"","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_004"}]}